### BookVerse — review-service (Lesson 05, Build 2).
### The seam: reviews are their own service, and a bookId is taken on trust.
### Covers the happy path, the ownership checks, the one-review-per-book
### constraint, and what happens when book-service is gone.
###
### Run requests.http first — you need a logged-in user and a real book id.
### Requires the "REST Client" VS Code extension.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000
@email = amr@example.com
@password = password123

### 1. Log in — reviews writes need a real identity
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###
@accessToken = {{login.response.body.data.tokens.accessToken}}

### 2. Create a book to review (owner = the logged-in user)
# @name createBook
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{
  "title": "The Seam",
  "description": "A book that exists, so we can review it.",
  "genre": ["non-fiction"]
}

###
@bookId = {{createBook.response.body.data.id}}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------
### HAPPY PATH
### ---------------------------------------------------------------

### 3a. POST /reviews — create. → 201
# @name createReview
POST {{baseUrl}}/reviews
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "bookId": "{{bookId}}",
  "rating": 4,
  "comment": "Held together better than expected."
}

###
@reviewId = {{createReview.response.body.data.id}}

### 3b. GET /reviews?bookId= — PUBLIC, no token. → 200, one review
GET {{baseUrl}}/reviews?bookId={{bookId}}

### 3c. GET /reviews/:id — PUBLIC. → 200
GET {{baseUrl}}/reviews/{{reviewId}}

### 3d. PATCH /reviews/:id — OWNER edits own review. → 200
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/reviews/{{reviewId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "rating": 5,
  "comment": "Revised upward after a second read."
}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------
### THE CONSTRAINT — one review per user per book
### ---------------------------------------------------------------

### 4. Same user, same book, second review. → 409 Conflict
### This is also why there is no Idempotency-Key on this route: a repeated
### submit conflicts instead of duplicating, so the retry is already safe.
POST {{baseUrl}}/reviews
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "bookId": "{{bookId}}",
  "rating": 1,
  "comment": "Trying to review the same book twice."
}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------
### VALIDATION
### ---------------------------------------------------------------

### 5a. rating out of range. → 400
POST {{baseUrl}}/reviews
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "bookId": "{{bookId}}",
  "rating": 9,
  "comment": "Off the scale."
}

### 5b. No token on a write. → 401
POST {{baseUrl}}/reviews
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "bookId": "{{bookId}}",
  "rating": 3
}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------
### THE SEAM — a bookId that points at nothing
### ---------------------------------------------------------------

### 6. Review a book that does not exist. → 201 with REVIEW_VERIFY_BOOK_EXISTS=false
###    This is the soft dependency, visible. The review is accepted and now
###    references nothing. With the flag "true" this is a 404 instead.
POST {{baseUrl}}/reviews
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "bookId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000",
  "rating": 5,
  "comment": "Reviewing a book that was never written."
}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------
### TRUST BOUNDARY (Lesson 01) — direct to review-service, no gateway secret
### ---------------------------------------------------------------
###
###   docker compose exec -T gateway wget -S -O- \
###     --header='Content-Type: application/json' \
###     --header='x-user-id: 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111' \
###     --header='x-user-role: USER' \
###     --header='x-user-email: forged@evil.test' \
###     --post-data='{"bookId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","rating":5}' \
###     http://review-service:3003/reviews 2>&1 | head -n 20
###
### Expect 401: the gateway is the only caller review-service accepts.

### ---------------------------------------------------------------
### BUILD 3 — measuring what the boundary costs
### ---------------------------------------------------------------
###
### Latency of the hard path:
###   set REVIEW_VERIFY_BOOK_EXISTS=true in .env.dev, `npm run compose`,
###   then run 3a repeatedly and compare `npm run latency:review` against
###   the same run with the flag false. Alternate arms in one sitting.
###
### Availability of the hard path:
###   docker compose stop book-service
###   then send 3a. With the flag false → 201. With it true → 503.
###   That gap is the whole hard-vs-soft argument, measured.

### 7. Cleanup — owner deletes own review. → 204
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/reviews/{{reviewId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
